      "voiceover": {
        "hook": "November the twenty-fifth, nineteen fifty-three. Wembley. England had never lost at home… to a team from the continent.",
        "context": "On one side, England. Sure of itself… its team still picked by a committee. On the other, Sebes's Hungary: a Stalinist regime, and the best players gathered in a single club, the army's. And in the stands… an Englishman. Jimmy Hogan. The man the Hungarians say taught them everything.",
        "players": "Puskás, the captain. Hidegkuti, a number nine… who drops deep where nobody expects him. And Bozsik, running the game.",
        "tactic": "Back then, every number marks a number. But Hidegkuti drops deep. If his marker follows, the defence opens up. If he stays, Hidegkuti is free. Nobody knows who to mark.",
        "goals": "The result: a goal inside two minutes. Then Puskás' drag-back, sending Billy Wright into thin air. And Hidegkuti, on the volley, for a hat-trick. Six-three.",
        "reach": "And this is only the beginning. Six months later, in Budapest: seven-one. England finally understands… and changes the way it trains. A year after Wembley, Wolves beat Honvéd. The English press cries “champions of the world”. In Paris, L'Équipe answers… by launching the European Cup. Manchester City copy Hidegkuti, and win the FA Cup. And Wembley's right-back, Alf Ramsey… gives England its only World Cup.",
        "epilogue": "Four years unbeaten. Until the World Cup final. Then came nineteen fifty-six. Revolution. Tanks. Puskás, Kocsis, Czibor… chose exile. The greatest team of its time was never world champion. But that night, at Wembley… it changed football.",
        "cta": "Every day, a match that changed everything. Follow for more."
      },
